1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,006
Your brain is lying to you right now. And\Nyou will never catch it in the act.

2
00:00:05,006 --> 00:00:11,140
Tonight: seven tricks your own mind plays on\Nyou daily.

3
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:20,610
Quick question. Have you ever been totally\Nsure about something, then learned you were\Ncompletely wrong?

4
00:00:20,610 --> 00:00:25,664
Hold that moment. It was never your fault.\NIt was your wiring.

5
00:00:25,664 --> 00:00:32,566
Psychologists have mapped these glitches for\Ndecades. They are called cognitive biases.

6
00:00:32,566 --> 00:00:40,236
Not flaws of stupid people. Doctors, judges,\Nand CEOs fall for them daily.

7
00:00:40,236 --> 00:00:48,458
A brilliant friend of mine got bent by these\Ntricks. Number seven is the one nobody talks\Nabout.

8
00:00:48,458 --> 00:00:53,512
Trick one. Confirmation bias. Running in\Nyour head right now.

9
00:00:53,512 --> 00:01:00,726
Your brain is no detective. It is a lawyer\Ndefending a verdict already reached.

10
00:01:00,726 --> 00:01:10,292
Decide something is true, and your mind\Nfilters reality. Agreement highlighted.\NDisagreement deleted.

11
00:01:10,292 --> 00:01:17,866
This is why arguments go nowhere. Both sides\Nfinished collecting proof before the talk\Nstarted.

12
00:01:17,866 --> 00:01:26,568
Two people watch one video and reach\Nopposite conclusions. The video did not\Nchange. The bias did.

13
00:01:26,568 --> 00:01:35,198
I have done this myself. Defended a diet\Nthat clearly failed, ignoring my own\Ndoctor's studies.

14
00:01:35,198 --> 00:01:42,700
It feels like thinking, like research. But\Nit is your brain admiring its own\Nreflection.

15
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:51,594
What evidence would change my mind? If\Nnothing could, that is a bias, not a belief.

16
00:01:51,594 --> 00:01:57,680
Now, the trick that empties bank accounts\Nand traps people in miserable jobs.

17
00:01:57,680 --> 00:02:06,022
Trick two. The sunk cost fallacy. You paid\Ntoo much to quit, so you keep paying.

18
00:02:06,022 --> 00:02:13,380
Ten minutes into an awful movie, you stay.\NBecause leaving would waste the ticket.

19
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:21,818
People stay in dead relationships for years.\NAs if one more year refunds the rest.

20
00:02:21,818 --> 00:02:30,088
Executives pour millions into failing\Nprojects, because admitting loss feels worse\Nthan losing more.

21
00:02:30,088 --> 00:02:37,398
Quitting feels like failure. Your ego\Nprefers losing more to admitting it lost.

22
00:02:37,398 --> 00:02:44,660
This one hit close to home. I once spent two\Nyears building something nobody wanted.

23
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:52,882
Casinos run on this. Every gambler thinks: I\Ncannot leave now, I have already lost so\Nmuch.

24
00:02:52,882 --> 00:03:00,600
Would you still choose this starting fresh?\NIf not, the past is an excuse.

25
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:07,068
The past is a sunk cost. The future is still\Nnegotiable.

26
00:03:07,068 --> 00:03:14,738
Trick three hijacks your sense of value\Nitself. Before anyone even names a price.

27
00:03:14,738 --> 00:03:22,408
Trick three. Anchoring. The first number you\Nhear becomes the reference for everything\Nafter it.

28
00:03:22,408 --> 00:03:29,814
Marked down from one thousand to two\Nhundred, any jacket feels like a steal. Even\None worth one fifty.

29
00:03:29,814 --> 00:03:39,212
In every negotiation, whoever names the\Nfirst number wins. It bends the whole\Nconversation around itself.

30
00:03:39,212 --> 00:03:46,714
Job interviews run on this. The first offer\Nsets the ceiling. Counteroffers just orbit\Ntheir anchor.

31
00:03:46,714 --> 00:03:53,352
Even a random wheel spin changes what people\Npay. Your brain anchors to noise.

32
00:03:53,352 --> 00:04:02,918
Retailers plant anchors daily. The\Ncrossed-out price was never real. It exists\Nto make the real price feel small.

33
00:04:02,918 --> 00:04:09,916
Set your number before seeing theirs. Bring\Nyour own anchor, or swim in theirs.

34
00:04:09,916 --> 00:04:16,600
Whoever names the first number controls the\Ntable. Remember that.

35
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:23,646
Trick four: losing one hundred hurts more\Nthan winning one hundred feels good.

36
00:04:28,148 --> 00:04:36,274
Trick four. Loss aversion. Losses feel twice\Nas painful as equal gains feel pleasant.

37
00:04:36,274 --> 00:04:43,536
Find a hundred, smile for an hour. Lose a\Nhundred, think about it for a week.

38
00:04:43,536 --> 00:04:54,230
Free trials run on this brutally well. Once\Nthe trial ends, canceling feels like losing\Nsomething you owned.

39
00:04:54,230 --> 00:05:03,844
Past halfway. The next three run your social\Nfeed. Brain workout? Hit subscribe. Dark\NMind Files, daily.

40
00:05:03,844 --> 00:05:12,114
FOMO is loss aversion in a party mask. Your\Nbrain hears: act now, or lose forever.

41
00:05:12,114 --> 00:05:20,600
Investors panic-sell at the bottom, then buy\Nback at the top. Loss aversion, charging a\Nfee every time.

42
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:28,174
Your closet proves it. You keep things you\Nnever use, because tossing them feels like\Nlosing.

43
00:05:28,174 --> 00:05:34,668
Nothing lost, only changed. You are not\Nlosing a subscription. You are freeing\Nmoney.

44
00:05:34,668 --> 00:05:45,048
Your brain treats losses like threats. You\Nare not in danger, just uncomfortable.\NDiscomfort passes.

45
00:05:45,048 --> 00:05:52,910
Trick five decides what scares you and what\Nyou argue about. It starts with your news\Nfeed.

46
00:05:52,910 --> 00:06:00,988
Trick five. The availability heuristic. Your\Nbrain judges likelihood by ease of recall.

47
00:06:00,988 --> 00:06:09,378
Your memory is not a database. It is a\Nhighlight reel. Drama gets front-row seats.

48
00:06:09,378 --> 00:06:17,144
You fear planes more than cars, though the\Nairport drive is deadlier. Memorable is not\Nlikely.

49
00:06:17,144 --> 00:06:24,766
You dread sharks, but vending machines kill\Nmore people. One makes movies. The other\Nmakes nothing.

50
00:06:24,766 --> 00:06:33,876
The news manufactures availability. One rare\Ntragedy, replayed for a week, feels like a\Ndaily event.

51
00:06:33,876 --> 00:06:43,634
Smart people refuse to fly after one news\Ncycle, then text while driving. Fear follows\Nfootage, not facts.

52
00:06:43,634 --> 00:06:51,496
Lotteries sell availability. You see the one\NTV winner. Never the forty million losers.

53
00:06:51,496 --> 00:07:01,326
The antidote is boring: ask what the numbers\Nsay. Boring numbers beat dramatic memories\Nevery time.

54
00:07:01,326 --> 00:07:07,530
Memorable is not the same as likely. Tattoo\Nthat on the inside of your skull.

55
00:07:07,530 --> 00:07:15,656
Trick six kept your ancestors alive. Today\Nit sells tickets, trends, and political\Nmovements.

56
00:07:15,656 --> 00:07:23,422
Trick six. The bandwagon effect. Everyone is\Ndoing it, so your brain reads safety.

57
00:07:23,422 --> 00:07:30,684
You join the crowded restaurant. Not for the\Nfood. Forty strangers cannot be wrong. They\Ncan.

58
00:07:30,684 --> 00:07:39,170
Every bubble is the bandwagon with money on\Nit. Rising prices attract buyers. Then the\Nmusic stops.

59
00:07:39,170 --> 00:07:49,696
Fashion, trends, viral opinions. Same\Nmechanism. Popularity hijacks judgment\Nbefore it wakes up.

60
00:07:49,696 --> 00:08:00,438
It bends elections. Polls pull undecided\Nvoters toward the leader. People want the\Nwinner, not the platform.

61
00:08:00,438 --> 00:08:08,204
Online pile-ons? Nobody reads the original\Npost. They read the outrage and add their\Nstone.

62
00:08:08,204 --> 00:08:14,290
What would you choose if nobody knew? If the\Nanswer changes, the crowd chose for you.

63
00:08:14,290 --> 00:08:20,758
Popularity is not proof. It is peer pressure\Nwearing a costume.

64
00:08:20,758 --> 00:08:27,900
Trick seven is the quietest. You will spot\Nit in every interview, date, and courtroom.

65
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:36,074
Trick seven. The halo effect. One good\Ntrait, and your brain assumes the rest.

66
00:08:36,074 --> 00:08:43,984
Attractive people get judged kinder and\Nsmarter. Same resume, different face,\Ndifferent verdict.

67
00:08:43,984 --> 00:08:54,222
Celebrities sell perfume and crypto alike.\NThe halo of fame smears onto the product.

68
00:08:54,222 --> 00:09:03,068
First impressions are halo factories. A firm\Nhandshake and a good suit outweigh an empty\Nresume.

69
00:09:03,068 --> 00:09:14,266
I once hired someone brilliant at discussing\Nthe work. Discussion was their only skill.\NThe halo blinded me.

70
00:09:14,266 --> 00:09:25,272
Its evil twin, the horn effect: one bad\Ntrait paints everything dark. One awkward\Nmoment sinks a candidate.

71
00:09:25,272 --> 00:09:31,598
Judge traits separately. Charm gets one\Nscore, competence another. Never average\Nthem.

72
00:09:31,598 --> 00:09:38,474
Charm is a spotlight. Always check what is\Nhiding in the shadows.

73
00:09:38,474 --> 00:09:50,200
Seven tricks, now with names: confirmation\Nbias, sunk cost, anchoring, loss aversion,\Navailability, bandwagon, halo.

74
00:09:50,200 --> 00:09:58,182
You cannot delete these glitches. But spot\None in the act, and it loses its power.

75
00:09:58,182 --> 00:10:06,980
Rewired how you see your own mind? Subscribe\Nto Dark Mind Files. Tomorrow: the seven\Ntricks others use on you.

